  "markdown": "## TL;DR\n\nLong needs-input diagnostics are now compact on both the board card and the drawer's \"Waiting for you\" block. Each detail renders as a full-width button clamped to three lines, and activating it opens the task on Debug → Status details, where the complete, unclamped message is shown. The change reuses the existing status-detail model and `openTaskStatusDetails`/`openStatusDetails` navigation, adds no colours, and keeps the primary action button visible below the clamped text. A focused component test covers the clamp, the full Debug text, and card-click navigation; two declared shots use dedicated check-wait fixtures.\n\n## Work and rationale\n\nThe worktree already contained a WIP self-check checkpoint with the core direction in place: the card detail was turned from a passive `<p>` into a button, the drawer detail gained a clamp, shared styles were added, and check-wait shot fixtures existed. I traced each piece through its callers before continuing, then tightened the parts that would have failed review or the gate.\n\nThe interaction deliberately reuses the existing status-detail path rather than building a parallel one. `openTaskStatusDetails` already opens the task, sets `ui.activeTab = \"debug\"`, and sets `ui.debugView = \"status\"`; the drawer's `openStatusDetails` does the same for an already-open task. The card button stops click propagation so the card's own click handler does not open the drawer on the default tab first. Because both surfaces are native `<button>` elements, Enter and Space activation come from the browser; I removed an earlier set of explicit keydown handlers that would have invoked the handler twice per key press. Visible focus rings use `var(--ring)`, the theme-aware token, with an outline offset.\n\nThe long text is kept in the DOM rather than rewritten. `formatNeedsInputDetailForDisplay` still decides whether a detail is useful, and `needsInputDetailUsesLineClamp` now reflects exactly that decision instead of a hard-coded reason list. CSS `-webkit-line-clamp: 3` on the card and in the drawer limits the rendered height while the full string remains available to assistive technology and to the Debug tab. The clamping applies only to the detail line, not the banner or action row, so \"Restart work\", \"Review again\" and the other primary actions stay in place.\n\nFor the Debug surface, the existing \"Waiting for you\" status row already includes `activeNeedsInputDetailLine`, which is where the full check-wait message lives. I kept the additional \"Check slot wait\" row sourced from `localQueueHintForTask`: a plain non-escalated queue wait has its long message in the hint title rather than `needsInputDetail`, so the explicit row makes \"click the clamped text, see the full text in Debug\" true for that state too.\n\nThe queue hint label for the memory-pressure case was shortened to `waiting · pressure · <duration>` while its title keeps the full reason. A long pressure message belongs in the diagnostic/title, not in the status chip, and shortening it supports the same predictable-card-height goal.\n\n## Approaches tried\n\nI first reviewed the WIP diff rather than assuming it was complete. That surfaced four issues worth fixing before handoff. First, the new helper accepted `string | null | undefined` but passed it straight to a function typed `string | undefined`; the remote gate caught this as `TS2345` at build time. It now normalizes with `detail ?? undefined`.\n\nSecond, the drawer action used bare-element selectors (`button.agent-waiting-detail`) for its reset styles. The `css-layers` guard correctly rejected those as unlayered element selectors that can beat Tailwind utilities. Rather than wrapping them in `@layer base`, I introduced a dedicated `.agent-waiting-detail--action` class on the button, which is narrower and keeps the existing `.agent-waiting-detail` rule untouched for the unrelated archived-reason div.\n\nThird, the new focus outlines referenced `var(--focus)`, which is not defined in the stylesheet (several older rules share that silent bug). I switched the new rules to `var(--ring)`, which is defined for every theme scope and is contrast-checked.\n\nFourth, the declared drawer shot routed to `/work?task=1034` while its `drawer:checkWaitDetail` fixture seeds the standard `9999` shot-preview task. The capture would have opened the real task without the fixture's long message, so the highlight and the clamped demo could disagree with the fixture. I corrected the declared route to `/work?task=9999` through `repoos update --shots`, keeping the card shot on `/` with the same fixture.\n\nI considered clamping the hint label itself or truncating the string in JavaScript. Both were rejected: truncating data hides the truth from the Debug tab, and clamping the whole waiting card would risk pushing or hiding the action controls. Isolating the clamp to a dedicated detail control keeps the action area stable.\n\nI also considered reseeding the fixture under the real `1034` id, but that couples preview state to a live task. The established convention for drawer fixtures is the `9999` fixture task plus a matching route, so I followed it.\n\n## Challenges and blockers\n\nThe only integration friction was the scoped gate itself. The first run failed on the type error in `needsInputUsesLineClamp`; after fixing it and rebuilding the UI, the next run failed on the CSS layering guard. Those were both real, deterministic problems rather than flakes, and each mapped to a specific guard rather than to machine load.\n\nThe remote changed-path run is the validation authority for this branch. `REPOOS_CHECK_CHANGED=main repoos check` now passes build, typecheck, lint, formatting, CSS layering, hardcoded-colour, theme and rendered contrast, bare-require, and the headless UI smoke test, with changed-path tests executed on the configured runner. No browser preview was requested because preview requests are the human's to make, not a routine handoff step.\n\nOne presentation trade-off: the inline \"Details\" affordance sits inside the clamped box, so a three-line message clips it too. The whole clamped text remains the activation target, carries an accessible label ending in \"Open Status details.\", and the card hint above it also routes to Status details, so the label is redundant rather than required. I kept the markup simple instead of adding an overlay or extra line.\n\n## Key learnings\n\nFor long operational diagnostics, the compact surface should preserve enough context to identify the problem while a direct, keyboard-accessible action exposes the full record. A CSS clamp is a good fit when the underlying text must stay complete and there is an existing detail view to route to.\n\nGuard failures are useful specifications. The `css-layers` message named the exact selectors and the fix (scope them or wrap them in `@layer base`), and the remote build error named the exact type mismatch. Reading the guard rather than working around it kept the change smaller and avoided weakening a shared rule.\n\nExisting code is not always a template. Several older rules use `var(--focus)`, but the token is undefined, so copying that pattern would have produced a focus ring that never renders. Checking the token table and using `var(--ring)` was the difference between a keyboard-accessible control on paper and one in the browser.\n\n## Generated change summary\n\nBase tree: `acd9edad502ec25daab5d0dbfbddddb50953efd2`\nSource tree: `31c0e3941918d7b9c532919b41b256c5791740ab`\n\n- M src/core/shot-fixtures.ts: +3 / −0\n- M src/ui-app/src/components/TaskCard.vue: +25 / −5\n- M src/ui-app/src/components/TaskDrawer.vue: +20 / −4\n- M src/ui-app/src/lib/local-queue-hint.ts: +7 / −1\n- M src/ui-app/src/lib/needs-input-ui.ts: +7 / −2\n- M src/ui-app/src/lib/shot-preview-fixtures.ts: +12 / −0\n- M src/ui-app/src/style.css: +37 / −2\n- M src/ui-app/tests/local-queue-hint.test.ts: +1 / −1\n- M src/ui-app/tests/needs-input-ui.test.ts: +4 / −3\n- M src/ui-app/tests/shot-preview-fixtures.test.ts: +10 / −0\n- A src/ui-app/tests/task-check-wait-detail-clamp.test.ts: +162 / −0"
